# Explore the Geometry of Search

A changing search tree is best measured in units of information, not merely in levels. Start with the hands-on calculator below, then move from local profiles to phase composition and periodic geometry.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The bars compare successful logarithmic volume with total ambient volume. Try increasing one successful coordinate while leaving the ambient profile fixed; the dimension can only rise.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the entropy power law</summary>
For positive profiles, path counts satisfy $P(a)=\prod_i a_i$ and logarithmic volumes satisfy $L(a)=\sum_i\log a_i=\log P(a)$. Consequently $P(s)=P(b)^{D(b,s)}$ for $D(b,s)=L(s)/L(b)$.
</details>

## Where does the information live?

{{visualization:0}}

The levelwise display makes clear why a high-branching level contributes more than a binary level. See the background ideas of [information theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_theory) and [fractal dimension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fractal_dimension).

{{algorithm:0}}

## Compose distinct phases

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

The correct combined dimension restores each phase's denominator, adds successful volumes, and normalizes once at the end.

<details><summary>Reveal the multiscale composition proof</summary>
Write $A_j=L(b^{(j)})$ and $d_j=L(s^{(j)})/A_j$. Additivity gives total successful volume $A_1d_1+A_2d_2$ and total ambient volume $A_1+A_2$. Their quotient is the ambient-entropy-weighted mean.
</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

## Periodic binary geometry

{{interactive_demo:2}}

In a binary period of $m$ levels, if $r$ levels retain two successful branches and all others retain one, then the dimension is exactly $r/m$.

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why repetition changes nothing</summary>
Repeating a block $k$ times multiplies both logarithmic volumes by $k$. The factor cancels, so dimension is scale invariant.
</details>

## Diagnose bottlenecks

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

A phase's entropy deficit is $L(b)-L(s)$. It measures absolute logarithmic pruning, while dimension measures retained volume relative to ambient volume. Use both when deciding where guidance is most valuable.

<details><summary>Prerequisites and limits</summary>
The product-profile model assumes levelwise homogeneous branching. General irregular trees may require prefix counts, subadditive limits, or transition matrices. Dimension measures abundance; it does not determine the order in which a traversal policy encounters successful nodes. For related infinite constructions, consult [Hausdorff dimension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hausdorff_dimension) and [symbolic dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbolic_dynamics).
</details>
